  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "The concept and its witnesses are written, but the gate hasn't judged them yet. Both runs I used (of 6) returned `SCOPE_VIOLATION` because of a harness problem, not because of the two files.\n\n**What blocked the gate:** each run flagged a file under `.runtime/tmp/claude-…/tasks/<id>.output`, and the name was different each time. That is the Bash tool's capture file for the gate command itself, written while the gate is running. I can't avoid or remove those files, so I stopped instead of spending the rest of the budget. The fix is for the gate's scope check to ignore `.runtime/`. The harness re-runs the gate after the session ends, and that will be the first verdict on the files themselves.\n\n**The concept body** (`TrafficSpec/Concepts/lane/entering_adjacent_lane.lean`):\n```\nst_lane_adjacency(r1, s, r2) ∧ rel_occupies(a, r1) ∧ rises(rel_occupies(a, r2) ∨ ¬rel_occupies(a, r1))\n```\n`rises` is the only way the language can refer to the previous observation, and the `rises` term is false there exactly when a was in r1 and not in r2. With a in r1 now, it is true now exactly when a is also in r2. Together that is the contract's condition. It can't hold twice without a leaving r2 in between, and it is unknown where occupancy or adjacency isn't observed, or at a trace's first observation. Both claimed atoms are used, and I didn't need any narrowings.\n\n**The witnesses:**\n- **Setup:** two northbound lanes, with 101 as 100's left neighbour, `s = left`, and each scenario judged at t = 1000 ms. Occupancy comes from the vehicle's footprint, as the trace checker requires.\n- **holds:** the vehicle is in lane 100 only, then across both lanes, then in 101 only.\n- **fails:** the vehicle stays in lane 100 only.\n- **blind:** the holds trace with `rel.occupies` masked.\n\nOne more thing was blocked: early on, a shell command to read Core files (`cd`, `sed`, `grep`) was denied because it needed approval. I read the same files with the Read tool instead, so nothing was lost.\n\n`task_result.md` in the working directory has the run log and the reasoning.",
